#: ../../source/tutorials/shapes_ellipsoid_s.ipynb:10004
msgid "Superellipsoids with Different Shape Parameters"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/shapes_ellipsoid_s.ipynb:10006
msgid ""
"The examples below illustrate how different shape parameters affect the "
"geometry of a generalized ellipsoid."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/shapes_ellipsoid_s.ipynb:10008
msgid "A generalized ellipsoid is described by the following equation:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/shapes_ellipsoid_s.ipynb:10010
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"\n"
"f(x,y,z) \\;=\\; \\Bigl[\\Bigl(\\frac{x}{a}\\Bigr)^2\\Bigr]^{S_a}\n"
"+\\Bigl[\\Bigl(\\frac{y}{b}\\Bigr)^2\\Bigr]^{S_b}\n"
"+\\Bigl[\\Bigl(\\frac{z}{c}\\Bigr)^2\\Bigr]^{S_c}\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/shapes_ellipsoid_s.ipynb:10016
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"where $a$, $b$, and $c$ are the semi-axis lengths along the $x$-, $y$-, "
"and $z$-axes, respectively.   $S_a$, $S_b$, and $S_c$ are the shape "
"indices along the three axes.   We also define the three ellipticities as"
" $\\varepsilon_{ab} = 1 - \\frac{b}{a}$, $\\varepsilon_{ac} = 1 - "
"\\frac{c}{a}$, and $\\varepsilon_{bc} = 1 - \\frac{c}{b}$."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/shapes_ellipsoid_s.ipynb:20002
msgid "Initializing the Shape"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/shapes_ellipsoid_s.ipynb:20004
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"Here we mainly use the `StructureCore` class, which integrates coordinate"
" transformations ({py:class}`CoordinateBase "
"<gal3d.shape.coordinate.CoordinateBase>`) and geometry generation "
"({py:class}`GeometryBase <gal3d.shape.geometry.GeometryBase>`). By "
"setting different parameters, you can generate different three-"
"dimensional shapes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/shapes_ellipsoid_s.ipynb:20006
msgid ""
"You can inspect all available initialization options using "
"`StructureCore.available_options()`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/shapes_ellipsoid_s.ipynb:40002
msgid ""
"We choose `EulerShift` as the coordinate transformation and `Ellipsoid_S`"
" as the geometry. As you can see, `shape.parameters` contains 12 "
"parameters. In this tutorial, we focus on the last six parameters, which "
"describe the geometric shape itself."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/shapes_ellipsoid_s.ipynb:50002
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"\n"
"\\begin{array}{r c l}\n"
"\\mathbf{Parameters} & & \\\\\n"
"\\mathrm{x} & : & 0.000 \\in [-0.200, 0.200] \\\\\n"
"\\mathrm{y} & : & 0.000 \\in [-0.200, 0.200] \\\\\n"
"\\mathrm{z} & : & 0.000 \\in [-0.200, 0.200] \\\\\n"
"\\mathrm{ang1} & : & 0.000 \\in [-3.142, 3.142] \\\\\n"
"\\mathrm{ang2} & : & 0.000 \\in [-1.571, 1.571] \\\\\n"
"\\mathrm{ang3} & : & 0.000 \\in [-3.142, 3.142] \\\\\n"
"\\mathrm{a} & : & 3.000 \\in [0.100, \\infty] \\\\\n"
"\\mathrm{eps\\_ab} & : & 0.200 \\in [0.001, 0.999] \\\\\n"
"\\mathrm{eps\\_bc} & : & 0.500 \\in [0.001, 0.999] \\\\\n"
"\\mathrm{sa} & : & 1.000 \\in [0.200, 2.000] \\\\\n"
"\\mathrm{sb} & : & 1.000 \\in [0.200, 2.000] \\\\\n"
"\\mathrm{sc} & : & 1.000 \\in [0.200, 2.000]\n"
"\\end{array}\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/shapes_ellipsoid_s.ipynb:60002
msgid "The Two-Dimensional Case"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/shapes_ellipsoid_s.ipynb:60004
msgid ""
"We begin with the two-dimensional case. Here we use `generate_slice2D` to"
" obtain a 2D slice of the ellipsoid."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/shapes_ellipsoid_s.ipynb:60006
msgid ""
"The figure below shows how the ellipticity `eps_ab` and the shape indices"
" `sa` and `sb` affect the 2D shape."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/shapes_ellipsoid_s.ipynb:60008
msgid ""
"The values of `sa` and `sb` for each subplot are shown in the panel "
"titles. Within a given subplot, different values of `eps_ab` are "
"indicated by color."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/shapes_ellipsoid_s.ipynb:60010
msgid ""
"Larger shape-index values make the shape boxier, while smaller values "
"make it more diamond-like or even star-like."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/shapes_ellipsoid_s.ipynb:80002
msgid "The Three-Dimensional Case"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/shapes_ellipsoid_s.ipynb:80004
msgid ""
"Using `generate_edge3D`, we can obtain the three-dimensional boundary of "
"the ellipsoid and then visualize its shape with `plot_surface`."
msgstr ""

